---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const arxiv = "https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35806";
const github = "https://github.com/prdai/sfia-structured-skill-extraction";

const rows = [
  ["BM25 lexical", "0.128", "0.260", "0.875", "0.8 ms"],
  ["Dense + LLM rerank", "0.232", "0.181", "0.436", "6.2 s"],
  ["Zero-shot LLM", "0.176", "0.368", "0.895", "54 ms"],
  ["Single-agent RAG", "0.213", "0.526", "0.816", "167 s"],
  ["Three-agent crew", "0.212", "0.319", "0.717", "327 s"],
];
---

<Base
  title="Why this is different — SFIA Explorer"
  description="Level-aware skill extraction with agentic RAG: how the matcher works, what the paper found, and what the public demo changes."
>
  <article class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-5 py-10">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <p class="mb-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-muted-foreground">Method</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        Why this is different
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Most skill tools return a flat list of labels. This one predicts a
        responsibility level for every skill, and grounds each decision in the
        SFIA 9 description for that exact skill-level pair.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-10 text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">The task is structured prediction</h2>
        <p>
          Given free text such as a job advert, course outline, or role profile,
          the system returns pairs of <span class="font-mono text-xs">(skill, level)</span>{" "}
          where the skill is one of 147 SFIA 9 skills and the level is an integer
          from 1 to 7. A label such as “cloud architecture” says nothing about
          whether the person follows instructions, works autonomously, leads
          delivery, or sets strategy. SFIA encodes that dimension; a flat skill
          list throws it away.
        </p>
        <p>
          The corpus behind the demo is 669 skill-level descriptions extracted
          automatically from the public SFIA 9 documentation. Every record pairs
          one skill with the verbatim description of that skill at one level.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">One agent, two retrievers</h2>
        <p>
          The matcher is a single agent with two search tools: semantic search
          for meaning-based matches, and keyword search for jargon, acronyms and
          named technologies. The agent chooses its own queries and can search
          repeatedly before committing to an answer. It sees the retrieved
          skill-level descriptions, not just skill names, so the level decision
          is grounded in evidence.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-foreground">semantic_skill_search</span>
            — vector retrieval over the skill-level descriptions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-foreground">keyword_skill_search</span>
            — BM25 retrieval for exact terms and named technologies.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border bg-card">
          <img
            src="/fig-arch-single.png"
            alt="Architecture of the single-agent RAG matcher: the agent queries semantic and keyword retrieval tools over the SFIA skill-level corpus, then returns skill-level matches."
            class="w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="border-t px-4 py-3 text-xs text-muted-foreground">
            Paper architecture. The demo keeps the loop, both retrieval tools
            and the reranker, replacing Qdrant with Vectorize.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">What the evaluation found</h2>
        <p>
          Five strategies were scored on 30 expert-mapped European ICT role
          profiles, using the same corpus and harness. Skill F1 ignores levels;
          level metrics are computed only on skills the matcher named correctly.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
            <thead class="bg-muted/50 text-muted-foreground">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Strategy</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Skill F1</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Level exact</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Within 1</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">s / role</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {rows.map((row) => (
                <tr class="border-t">
                  {row.map((cell, index) => (
                    <td class:list={["px-3 py-2", index === 0 ? "font-medium" : "tabular-nums text-muted-foreground"]}>
                      {cell}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">
          Paper results, each strategy at its swept configuration. The public
          demo uses <span class="font-mono">nemotron-3-120b-a12b</span>, the
          fastest model in the single-agent sweep (0.230 F1 at 9.1 s per role in
          that sweep), rather than the paper's final GLM-4.7-Flash run above.
          Run-to-run variance in this harness is several points, so read close
          numbers as ties.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">The negative result</h2>
        <p>
          Splitting the work across a retriever, matcher and verifier looks
          appealing, but it did not help here. The three-agent crew matched the
          single agent on skill F1 (0.212 versus 0.213) at roughly double the
          latency (327 versus 167 seconds per role), with markedly worse level
          fidelity (MAE 1.16 versus 0.68). Passing evidence through conversation
          text lost the grounding the single agent gets from reading tool output
          directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why the public demo is the single agent. More roles do not
          automatically mean better closed-taxonomy prediction.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">What the demo changes</h2>
        <p>
          The paper's implementation is Python and CrewAI, with local Qdrant and
          a 20-candidate LLM reranker. The public demo is a port that keeps the
          prompt, both retrieval tools, the reranker and the structured output,
          but runs on Cloudflare Workers with Vectorize and Workers AI. One
          vector per skill-level record, the same embedding model, the same BM25
          scoring, and the same retrieval constants.
        </p>
        <p>
          That makes the deployed configuration a variant of the evaluated one.
          Before citing demo numbers, the ported configuration should be re-run
          on the same 30-role harness. The paper, code, corpus and evaluation
          harness are all public.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">Limits</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
          <li>The evaluation set is 30 one-sentence role summaries. Longer inputs may behave differently.</li>
          <li>Ground truth predates SFIA 9, so 31% of gold pairs are unreachable and recall is capped at 0.69.</li>
          <li>Level metrics condition on correctly named skills, so denominators differ across strategies.</li>
          <li>The demo is rate-limited to three analyses per person per day and has a monthly budget cap.</li>
          <li>Skill pages show short excerpts and link to the official SFIA pages; they are not a substitute for the framework.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 border-t pt-6">
        <a
          class="inline-flex h-9 items-center rounded-2xl bg-foreground px-4 text-sm font-medium text-background transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
          href={arxiv}>Read the paper</a
        >
        <a
          class="inline-flex h-9 items-center rounded-2xl border px-4 text-sm font-medium transition-colors hover:bg-muted"
          href={github}>View source and corpus</a
        >
        <a
          class="inline-flex h-9 items-center rounded-2xl border px-4 text-sm font-medium transition-colors hover:bg-muted"
          href="/cite/">Cite this</a
        >
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
